Mild Seropositive Rheumatoid Arthritis (Palindromic)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Episodic swelling of small joints (hands/feet), positive RF/CCP, no joint damage

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-032
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe monoarticular swelling and pain in the foot requiring exclusion of acute gout or septic arthritis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, intense, rapid-onset joint swelling in the foot. Immediate clinical differentiation from an acute septic joint or crystalline arthropathy (gout) was required to determine the need for emergency joint aspiration and intravenous antibiotics prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My left ankle and top of my foot suddenly ballooned up a few hours ago, the pain is agonizing, and I can't even put weight on it.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports intermittent, fleeting joint inflammation historically. Tonight, the patient experienced a very rapid onset of severe pain, swelling, and extreme stiffness in the left midfoot and ankle, evolving over 4 hours. The patient has been unable to bear weight and is fearful of a severe bone infection or a blood clot. Denies trauma, fever, or prior episodes of classic podagra (first MTP gout).
Functional Status: Currently non-weight bearing on the left leg, mobilizing by hopping.
Social/Safety Concerns: High falls risk due to altered mobility.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate acute pain, sitting with the left foot elevated.
Physical Exam: The left midfoot and ankle show localized, moderate soft-tissue swelling and mild erythema. Exquisite tenderness to direct palpation over the joint lines. Active and passive range of motion of the ankle is restricted by pain. The first MTP joint is notably spared. No ascending lymphangitis. Right foot is completely normal. History confirms episodic swelling of small joints (hands/feet), positive RF/CCP, no joint damage, characteristic of palindromic rheumatism.
Environmental Observations: Living room is cluttered; cleared a safe pathway for the patient.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has not taken any analgesia tonight.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Palindromic Rheumatism Flare (Mild Seropositive Rheumatoid Arthritis) - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high fever, rigors, systemic toxicity, and lack of extreme pain on microscopic passive joint movement.

Acute Gouty Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sparing of the 1st MTP joint, milder erythema (gout is typically intensely red/shiny), and strong history of palindromic, migratory seropositive flares.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Swelling strictly localized to the joints rather than the entire calf, soft calf muscles, and no popliteal tenderness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (mobility limitation and falls risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe localized joint swelling mimicking acute gout or early septic arthritis. Triage correctly prioritized a physical examination to definitively exclude a septic joint requiring immediate hospital admission. Bedside evaluation confirmed a severe localized inflammatory flare consistent with the patient's known seropositive palindromic rheumatism, effectively ruling out systemic infection or a deep venous process. Immediate provision of robust anti-inflammatory therapy and a safe mobility plan was essential to control the acute pain crisis and safely avert a late-night emergency department presentation, strictly fulfilling the requirement for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered robust oral NSAID dose (e.g., naproxen 500mg) and initiated RICE (Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation) therapy. Provided crutches (if available/required) or instructed on safe assisted mobilization.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply an ice pack wrapped in a towel strictly for 15 minutes every 2 hours while keeping the foot elevated above the level of your heart. Aggressively chilling the joint will rapidly shut down the inflammatory blood vessels, providing immediate numbing relief and shrinking the swelling overnight.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48-72 hours to evaluate the resolution of the flare and consider initiation of hydroxychloroquine.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the swelling rapidly spreads up your leg, if the joint becomes agonizingly painful to even touch lightly, or if you develop a high fever with shaking chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending response to therapy; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Palindromic flares are intense but typically "burn out" and completely resolve within 48 to 72 hours without causing permanent joint damage.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the pain does not significantly improve after 24 hours of consistent anti-inflammatory use and strict elevation.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This is a "hit-and-run" immune flare targeting the joint lining. It is not an infection or crystal buildup. Strong anti-inflammatories and ice will force the immune attack to stop.
Actionable Steps: Do not attempt to walk normally on the foot tomorrow. Keep it strictly elevated. You must follow up with your GP to get medication that prevents these sudden attacks.

